Yes. And the first correction actually makes the thought experiment better: not all eels go to the Sargasso Sea. The Atlantic freshwater eels—European eel (Anguilla anguilla) and American eel (A. rostrata)—are the famous Sargasso migrants. Other anguillid eels have their own oceanic spawning regions; Japanese eels, for example, spawn near the West Mariana Ridge in the Pacific. The common pattern is therefore deeper than “Sargasso”: freshwater/coastal growth → transformation into a migratory form → enormous ocean journey → a geographically constrained spawning region positioned so the offspring enter useful current systems. 

For the Atlantic species, the Sargasso story is not merely folklore. Tiny, recently hatched larvae have repeatedly been found there, an American eel has been satellite-tracked toward the spawning region, and in 2022 European silver eels were tracked all the way into their presumed Sargasso breeding area. But we still have never directly watched European or American eels spawning there, and their exact final spawning behavior remains one of the great open problems in migration biology. 

The interesting question is therefore exactly yours:

What does the Atlantic look like from inside an eel?

Probably almost nothing like a human map.

I would model their navigational world as a stack of overlapping fields rather than objects and place names.

Imagine the eel does not internally represent:

“I am off Ireland at 48° N, 18° W; Bermuda is that way.”

Instead its nervous system may represent something closer to:

magnetic field strength = X
magnetic inclination = Y
current = from this direction
temperature = falling/rising
pressure/depth = Z
photoperiod = shortening
tidal phase = this
internal reproductive state = this
therefore: swim approximately southwest/downstream/deeper tonight.

Repeat that rule millions of times and a continental animal can arrive thousands of kilometres away.

That is a profoundly different kind of “map.”

The magnetic landscape may be their equivalent of visible geography

There is good evidence that European eels possess magnetic orientation abilities. Juvenile European eels placed under experimentally altered geomagnetic conditions changed their direction appropriately; other experiments show glass eels can actually learn and retain the magnetic direction of tidal currents in their home estuary. 

This suggests a fascinating possibility.

Earth’s magnetic field has structure. Its intensity and inclination change geographically. To us those gradients are invisible. To a magnetically sensitive animal, however, the Atlantic might contain something analogous to enormous invisible hills, valleys and contour lines.

So where we see:

featureless water → featureless water → featureless water

the eel may experience:

this magnetic neighborhood → that magnetic neighborhood → another characteristic field configuration.

Not necessarily conscious perception. More like the way you know which way is upright because your vestibular system continuously resolves gravity without making you calculate 9.81\,m/s^2.

That gives us an important distinction:

world model ≠ conscious picture of the world.

A world model can be encoded in sensorimotor policies.

⸻

And it may not even require true point-to-point navigation.

One modeling study asked exactly this. Researchers compared hypothetical eels possessing a continuously updated geographical map against eels simply maintaining an inherited compass heading. Surprisingly, because the Sargasso spawning region is enormous, a relatively simple heading strategy could get them into the appropriate region; random swimming or passive drifting generally could not. 

So the navigation algorithm could be hierarchical:

Phase 1 — escape.
Freshwater flow, tides and remembered estuarine cues tell it how to leave its river.

Phase 2 — acquire bearing.
Once offshore, an inherited magnetic compass says roughly southwest for a European eel or south/southeast depending on origin for American eels.

Phase 3 — ocean crossing.
Maintain a heading while currents displace you; periodically correct from geomagnetic information.

Phase 4 — recognize the neighborhood.
Magnetic signature + temperature + salinity/current structure + perhaps chemical/bathymetric cues indicate “you have entered the right basin.”

Phase 5 — rendezvous.
Once eel density increases enormously, local chemical, hydrodynamic and perhaps pheromonal information could solve the last kilometres.

That is remarkably economical.

You don’t need an Atlantic map.

You need a sequence of policies that transforms the problem as the problem gets smaller.

And there is evidence that eels do exactly this kind of contextual switching. Migrating silver eels change vertical behavior dramatically offshore: at night they tend to occupy roughly the upper mesopelagic region, and during daylight descend hundreds of metres—sometimes toward 600–1000 m. Their movement is closely tied to sunrise/sunset, and some movements also synchronize with tides. 

So their map isn’t even merely horizontal.

It may be a four-dimensional landscape:

\text{place} =
f(B,\nabla B,T,S,P,\vec C,\text{light},\text{tide},t,\text{internal state})

where:

* B = geomagnetic field,
* T = temperature,
* S = salinity,
* P = pressure/depth,
* \vec C = water-current vector,
* t = circadian/seasonal/lunar time.

A place is recognized by a configuration of variables, not by an image.

Your Mecca analogy is useful—but with one major difference

As far as we know, Atlantic anguillid eels aren’t reproducing normally elsewhere and then undertaking an optional pilgrimage to the Sargasso.

The migration appears tied directly to sexual maturation. They transform from the feeding yellow eel phenotype into the migratory silver eel phenotype, stop behaving like ordinary feeding residents, leave continental waters, migrate thousands of kilometres, mature sexually during the journey, reproduce, and are generally thought to die afterward.

So “Mecca” understates it.

It’s closer to:

the pilgrimage is also puberty, mating season, migration, and the terminal act of the life cycle.

There isn’t evidence for a parallel “just mate locally in Virginia and make the pilgrimage when convenient” strategy in American eels. American eels are considered effectively one interbreeding population despite occupying an enormous continental range. 

Which produces a strange consequence: an eel from a tiny creek in Virginia and one from Canada can spend years in wildly different environments and yet belong to the same oceanic reproductive system.

And this answers your question about how the knowledge is “shared.”

It probably isn’t shared primarily eel-to-eel.

Much of the information may be carried by three things:

genes + environment + developmental state.

That is, evolution does not need to transmit:

“Here are directions to the Sargasso Sea.”

It can transmit something much smaller:

When physiology reaches state M, respond to cue A with behavior B.
When cue C crosses threshold x, switch behavior.
When magnetic configuration resembles D, alter heading.
When darkness begins, ascend.
When light returns, descend.

Nature stores the rest of the “map” outside the animal.

That is the part I think you were sniffing out.

The ocean itself becomes external memory.

The magnetic field stores orientation information.
Currents store transport structure.
Seasonality stores timing.
Tides store directional opportunity.
Temperature and salinity fronts store boundaries.
The eel only needs the receptors and transition rules required to read them.

So instead of carrying an enormous representation of reality internally:

\boxed{\text{small internal policy}+\text{structured environment}
\rightarrow\text{apparently enormous intelligence}}

That architecture appears everywhere.

A migrating animal doesn’t necessarily contain the route; animal + world contains the route.

And there is an even stranger recursive piece. Baby eels leave the Sargasso largely by exploiting Atlantic circulation; years later the adult effectively runs the transportation network backward. Juvenile European eels experimentally exposed to different magnetic signatures alter swimming directions in ways that models suggest would improve their entrainment into the Gulf Stream system. 

So selection may have tuned their magnetic responses not merely toward a location, but toward the dynamics of the ocean itself.

That means the actual inherited target may not be:

“Find coordinate X.”

It could be closer to:

“Find and ride the correct part of this dynamical system.”

And now the Sargasso makes much more intuitive sense. It is not necessarily some magical sacred point recognizable from thousands of kilometres away. It is a stable attractor in a coupled biological–magnetic–oceanographic system.

That distinction is powerful:

landmark navigation: find the thing.
coordinate navigation: find the position.
gradient navigation: descend the field.
vector navigation: maintain the heading.
flow navigation: enter the right transport stream.
state navigation: change policies when conditions change.
attractor navigation: follow locally available rules until they converge on the desired region.

The eel may be using several simultaneously.

And then something apparently miraculous—an animal departing a European river and reaching a remote patch of Atlantic Ocean it has never consciously “seen”—falls out of a set of comparatively simple interlocking loops.

The map is partly in the eel.
The map is partly in the ocean.
The journey emerges from the coupling between them.

That is probably the most productive abstraction here, because it scales well beyond eels.